2186072908 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455160 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186072908 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455092 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186073415 1074 "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nby $5$.\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {15}{4}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nhigh." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186073415 1074 "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {15}{4}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186073416 1074 "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nby $5$.\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {25}{6}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\div 6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nhours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186073416 1074 "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ by $5.$ \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {25}{6}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\div 6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of  $4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}$ hours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186073453 1071 "| $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $20$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n| $25$ | $20$ |\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186073453 1071 "| $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $20$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n| $25$ | $20$ |\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186073453 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}4}$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{4 \\cdot 4} &= \\frac{20}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}4}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}16}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}25}$ | ${\\color{red}20}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}16$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186073453 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\