umerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8\\times \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{8\\times 1}{4}=\\frac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{4}=8÷4=2\n$$\n\nSo, Ted used $2\\,\\text{lb}$ of cheese to make the pizzas." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186074445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186074445 1071 "A wooden slat is $3\\,\\text{ft}$ long. Eddie needs to cut a piece that's $\\frac{5}{8}$ the length of the slat. How long is the piece he has to cut?" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186074445 1071 "A wooden slat is $3ft$ long. Eddie needs to cut a piece that's $\\frac{5}{8}$ the length of the slat. How long is the piece he has to cut?" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186074445 1074 "To find the length of the piece that Eddie has to cut, we have to multiply $3$ by $\\frac{5}{8}.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3=\\frac{5\\times 3}{8}=\\frac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{15}{8}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15÷8=1\\,\\text{R}\\,7=1\\,\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, Eddie has to cut a piece $1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{ft}$ long." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186074957 1064 "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nhow many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nby $8$.\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} = 40 \\div 4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186074957 1064 "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb},$ how many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}$ by $8.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} =  40 \\div 4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186074968 1064 "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2}),$ $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}),$ $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8}).$\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0).$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186074968 1064 "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2})$, $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4})$, $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8})$.\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0)$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186075038 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n![](images/6c/6cda61e0fbd0d9695644a29f0c6fa3478eae84cf253623da7f4b544f35873472.svg)\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186075038 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n$$\n00\n$$\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10}$, which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}$. We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 2}{10 \\cdot 2} &= \\frac{10}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186119822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455442 131