{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-2)+2=0.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186120352 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-2)+2=0.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186124325 1071 "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/3d/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186124325 1071 "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/dc/dc7167e974da1de140ef435bef80635dce264d35149e4b47d11163da494f96e3.svg) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186124325 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the 