ed}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186125133 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 2x-4y \\\\ -2x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-8y=6 \\\\ 2x-4y=8 \\\\ -2x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8y & =6 \\\\ 3(4+2y)-8y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-6 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186125782 1064 "The **kernel** of a linear transformation is the set of vectors that map to the zero vector. We define it formally as follows:\n\n*For a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, the **kernel** of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the set of all the vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ We can write this as*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\left\\{ \\;\\mathbf{u} \\;| \\; \\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n