thbf{T}(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the linear transformation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that, for example, the vector $\\mathbf b = [6,9]^T$ is in the image of the transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ since there is another vector $\\mathbf{u} = [1,1]^T$ such that $\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{b}{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186125783 1064 "The **image** (or **range**) of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is the set of all vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ that the transformation maps to. That is to say\n![](images/4c/4c89132f0fd9713f0e688f7cf00189bf4d3f27baddad032c74edf7b0a8c0d8ee.svg)\n\nThe diagram below gives a schematic representation of the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: V \\rightarrow V$ for a vector space $V.$\n\n![](images/b8/b859250756eb3870f9c1d4d7e6e78f044adf2b0c4cd94a87d01011879d668afc.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{0}$ is always in the image of a linear transformation since $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the linear transformation\n![](images/61/618376922e3c837dba3f742fd77340c2a3deee6cee099843232f85bcec5faf93.svg)\n\nNotice that, for example, the vector $\\mathbf b = [6,9]^T$ is in the image of the transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ since there is another vector $\\mathbf{u} = [1,1]^T$ such that $\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{b}{:}$\n\n![](images/51/51004801582ad1a6cddc389e07a4ac8d486c410ebacf264de14ac1a798a10c30.svg)" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186125819 1064 "In general, the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to the column space of its standard matrix $T.$ That is,\n\n$$\nT\n$$\n\nSo to find out if a vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is in the image of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we just need to check if the matrix equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186125819 1064 "In general, the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to the column space of its standard matrix $T.$ That is,\n![](images/6e/6e8b0992ade86b8e8a0c7d2227ff5473f5cae843f8e763e556c17654e7f1f143.svg)\n\nSo to find out if a vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is in the image of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we just need to check if the matrix equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127130 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127131 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127132 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127133 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127134 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454911 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454970 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455036 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455069 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455178 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127129 1078 #edb/ref 17592186454911 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127136 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127137 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127138 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127139 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127140 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454912 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454932 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454996 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455123 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455146 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186127135 1078 #edb/ref 17592186454912 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129127 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455432 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129127 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455462 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129127 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455470 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129127 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455512 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129608 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186129608 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129608 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129609 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ =-1$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186129609 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186129609 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186147146 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/81/81b70e75c29649960c40e1fde55949f210718ebc3ea188686742605717512933.svg) of $20 = 35$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186147146 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n$\\qquad$ $0$ of $20 = 35$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186147146 1074 "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n![](images/44/44ceb7ccb604fc5f57bb5be3f77c564716f4bd9281c07d349d89fe298ae0e43a.svg)\n\nUsi