dot 20 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = {50}$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186151901 1074 "To find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $20,$ we apply a new percentage of  \n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting our percentage to a decimal:  \n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $20,$ we multiply $20$ by $2.5{:}$  \n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 20 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = {50}.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186151913 1074 "To find this year's gym membership, we apply a $400\\%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 400\\% = 500\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500\\% = \\dfrac{500}{100} = 5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n80 \\times 5 = 400\n$$\n\nSo, the gym has $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{400}$ members this year." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186151913 1074 "To find this year's gym membership, we apply a $400\\%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 400\\% = 500\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500\\% = \\dfrac{500}{100} = 5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n80 \\times 5 = 400\n$$\n\nSo, the gym has $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{400}$ members this year." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455436 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455452 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455490 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455497 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455508 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455443 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455445 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455453 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455471 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152162 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455435 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152162 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455484 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152162 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455509 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455458 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455469 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455451 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455478 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455495 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455498 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455476 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455482 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455431 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455511 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152315 1050 660.0 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152335 1050 780.0 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152739 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xyds$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r = 5\\sin θ$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186152739 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152739 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =25{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}25{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =125\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}({1}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152742 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = x + y$ along the polar curve $r = 2$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le 0$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186152742 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186152742 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =4([0-1]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186153325 1064 "Sometimes we need to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a so-called **percentage increase.**\n\nTo apply a percentage increase, we add the increase to $100\\%$, convert the result to a decimal multiplier, and then find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following problem.\n\n> What number is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$?\n\nThe key words here are \"$\\boldsymbol{25\\%}$ **greater than.**\" This means we need to apply a percentage increase, increasing the original number by $25\\%$.\n\nTo find a number $25\\%$ greater than $160$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 25\\% = 125\\%\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $125\\%$ of $160$.\n\nSo, first, we convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\% = \\dfrac{125}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nTo find a number that is $25\\%$ greater than $160$, we multiply $160$ by $1.25{:}$\n\n$$\n1.25 \\cdot 160 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$ is $200$.\n\nThis method applies to *any* percentage increase, including those greater than $100\\%{;}$ add the increase to $100\\%$ before converting to a decimal multiplier." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186153325 1064 "Sometimes we need to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a so-called **percentage increase.**\n\nTo apply a percentage increase, we add the increase to $100\\%,$ convert the result to a decimal multiplier, and then find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$\\boldsymbol{25\\%}$ **greater than.**\" This means we need to apply a percentage increase, increasing the original number by $25\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 25\\% = 125\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $125\\%$ of $160.$ \n\nSo, first, we convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\% = \\dfrac{125}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nTo find a number that is $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we multiply $160$ by $1.25{:}$\n\n$$\n1.25 \\cdot 160 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$ is $200.$\n\nThis method applies to *any* percentage increase, including those greater than $100\\%{;}$ add the increase to $100\\%$ before converting to a decimal multiplier." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186153340 1064 "It's possible to increase a number by $100\\%$ or more.\n\nFor example, what number is $150\\%$ greater than $90$?\n\nTo find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $90$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$, we multiply $90$ by $2.5$:\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 90 = 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$ is $225$." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186153340 1064 "It's possible to increase a number by $100\\%$ or more. \n\nFor example, what number is $150\\%$ greater than $90?$\n\nTo find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $90,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90,$ we multiply $90$ by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 90 = 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$ is $225.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186179740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455463 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186179740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455485 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186179740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455487 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186179740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455499 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186179741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455447 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191884 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191885 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191886 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191887 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191888 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454994 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455095 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186191883 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455165 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186191883 1075 #ed