e scale of numbers:\n\n![](images/8a/8a89b7b52135983b18892d07e173b95d7afdcc1efaa4b9c91fb0013b26d279fc.png)\n\nTherefore, we have that $m∠ABC={120}^{∘}.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186193345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186193345 1074 "Let's use the upper scale of the protractor.\n\n- The side $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ of the angle intersects the upper scale of numbers at ${145}^{∘}.$\n\n- The side $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ intersects the upper scale of numbers at ${65}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find the measure of $∠ABC,$ we subtract the smaller reading from the larger one and get\n\n$$\nm∠ABC={145}^{∘}-{65}^{∘}={80}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186193813 1064 "We use a **protractor** to measure angles. A protractor is an instrument that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c8865e67ff36fe06a6d1cf1be8c591468f1fef98b060ccb404b3bb61509edcf.png)\n\nTo find the measure of an angle using a protractor, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Place the midpoint of the protractor on the vertex of the angle.\n2. Align one side of the angle with the line passing through the midpoint and zero on the protractor.\n3. Read off the degrees where the other side of the angle intersects the scale.\n\nLet's measure the following angle using a protractor.\n\n![](images/ba/baebe10676f4e321f0e3983ffd848718f0a569a601f7e7fef36679f05984dd08.png)\n\nWe place the midpoint of our protractor on the vertex and align a zero on the protractor with one of the sides.\n\n![](images/5f/5f4de191857fa5b08e9b1675205149108ce0db0e46815a572cccddb665865f69.png)\n\nReading the protractor, we see that the angle measures $45^\\circ$.\n\n**Warning:** Protractors usually have two scales in opposite directions. Be careful which one you use. In this case, we used the *upper* scale because this is the one that starts from zero as we measure counterclockwise." 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186193813 1064 "We use a **protractor** to measure angles. A protractor is an instrument that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c8865e67ff36fe06a6d1cf1be8c591468f1fef98b060ccb404b3bb61509edcf.png)\n\nTo find the measure of an angle using a protractor, we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Place the midpoint of the protractor on the vertex of the angle.\n\n2\\. Align one side of the angle with the line passing through the midpoint and zero on the protractor.\n\n3\\. Read off the degrees where the other side of the angle intersects the scale.\n\nLet's measure the following angle using a protractor.\n\n![](images/ba/baebe10676f4e321f0e3983ffd848718f0a569a601f7e7fef36679f05984dd08.png)\n\nWe place the midpoint of our protractor on the vertex and align a zero on the protractor with one of the sides.\n\n![](images/5f/5f4de191857fa5b08e9b1675205149108ce0db0e46815a572cccddb665865f69.png)\n\nReading the protractor, we see that the angle measures $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Warning:** Protractors usually have two scales in opposite directions. Be careful which one you use. In this case, we used the *upper* scale because this is the one that starts from zero as we measure counterclockwise." 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230282 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455260 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230282 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455260 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230283 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454917 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230283 1078 #edb/ref 17592186454917 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230286 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230287 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230288 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230289 13194139535005 17592186045417 false] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454913 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454997 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186230285 1075 #edb/ref 1759